Home and small-business security cameras are starting to move. Ring, the camera brand owned by Amazon, is reported to have announced two robots in November 2026: a small indoor drone and a wheeled patrol robot aimed at businesses. This post sets out what has been reported, what is still unknown, and what to check before you consider buying either one.

*Disclosure: this post was drafted by Claude, an AI assistant made by Anthropic. It has not yet had an independent AI review, and it is not a full human review. Stackbrook did not test either product and has no affiliate relationship with any company mentioned at the time of writing. The cover image is AI-generated.*

## What has been reported

According to a newsletter summary dated 11 November 2026, Ring announced two products that both rely on what it calls an AI Pilot to map rooms and suggest where a robot should patrol. The figures below are as reported and were not confirmed on Ring's own page in this run.

The Drone Cam is described as an indoor drone that stays docked with its camera off. When an alarm trips, it flies to preset patrol points and records 2K video. It is reported at $249.99, with shipping planned for 27 January 2027, and the listing is said to be pending authorization from the US Federal Communications Commission. Scheduled patrol routes are reported to need a Ring Protect subscription.

The Rover Cam is described as a patrol robot for business sites, with a periscope-style mast, a siren, a light tower and two-way audio. It is reported at $999.99, with preorders open and shipping from 16 December 2026. One section of the newsletter mentions 4K recording while moving, but the main description gives no resolution, so that figure is left out of the quick answer.

![Horizontal bars comparing the reported prices of Ring Drone Cam at 249.99 dollars and Rover Cam at 999.99 dollars, with reported ship dates](/images/site1/ring-drone-cam-rover-cam-security-robots-what-is-reported-chart.svg)
*Chart created by Stackbrook from the cited pages.*

## Why the camera stays off until an alarm

The reported design choice that matters most for privacy is that the Drone Cam keeps its camera switched off while docked. That means it is not recording a living room day to day; it only wakes when something triggers an alarm. If that holds in practice, it is a different trade-off from a fixed indoor camera that watches continuously. Whether the camera light, the recording rules and the storage settings work the way the summary suggests is something only Ring's own documentation can settle.

## What is not known yet

Several things that would matter to a buyer are missing from the summaries we could read. We do not know the flight time per charge, how long recharging takes, how the drone avoids obstacles or pets, or how loud it is. We do not know how long video is stored, whether the subscription is required for basic alarm response or only for scheduled routes, or what the monthly cost is. For the Rover Cam, there is no reported battery life, terrain limit, payload or insurance guidance. Neither product has an independent review, because the Drone Cam has not shipped and the Rover Cam ships only in December.

The FCC point deserves attention. A device that is pending authorization can have its date slip or its design change if the regulator asks for adjustments. Anyone planning around the 27 January date should treat it as a target rather than a promise.

## Questions a small business should ask before buying

Even at a reported $999.99, a patrol robot is a bigger commitment than a camera. Before preordering, ask whether your premises are suitable for a wheeled robot, such as flat floors and no stairs, and whether your insurer or landlord has views on autonomous devices. Check what the siren and two-way audio mean for local noise rules. Look at where video is stored and for how long, and who at your company can see it. If the robot films employees or customers, privacy and workplace-monitoring rules may apply where you are; this is general information, not legal advice, so ask a qualified adviser.

It is also reasonable to compare against simpler options. A fixed camera with motion alerts costs less and has a known track record. A robot is interesting mainly if you need to cover several areas with a single device.

## What to watch next

The useful next steps are straightforward. Read Ring's own product and support pages for both devices, confirm the prices and dates there, and look for the subscription terms in writing. Watch for the FCC authorization to be granted, and wait for hands-on reviews from independent outlets once the Rover Cam ships in December. We will update this post with an official-source check before it is published.
